---
import SupervisorLog from "../components/SupervisorLog.astro";
import Terminal from "../layouts/Terminal.astro";
import { routeExcerpt } from "../data/routes";

const tree = [
	{ prefix: "", name: "floodgate", desc: null },
	{ prefix: "├─", name: "socketio_transport", desc: "official Fluid/Routerlicious wire format" },
	{ prefix: "├─", name: "phx_transport", desc: "Phoenix Channels wire format (levee-driver compatible)" },
	{ prefix: "├─", name: "beryl", desc: "supervised socket actors, channel routing, pubsub, presence" },
	{ prefix: "│ └─", name: "document_channel", desc: "auth, sequencing, signals, summaries" },
	{ prefix: "├─", name: "storage", desc: "per-tenant backend" },
	{ prefix: "│ ├─", name: "shelf (dets)", desc: "default \u2014 persistent, one file per document" },
	{ prefix: "│ ├─", name: "ets", desc: "in-memory, process-local" },
	{ prefix: "│ └─", name: "memory", desc: "ephemeral, used by tests" },
	{ prefix: "└─", name: "spillway", desc: "protocol: message types, sequencing, validation" },
];

const composedFrom = [
	{ name: "spillway", role: "Fluid protocol: message types, sequencing, validation, signals, nacks" },
	{ name: "beryl", role: "supervised socket runtime, channel routing, pubsub, and presence" },
	{ name: "signet", role: "JWT signing and verification" },
	{ name: "silt", role: "Git object encoding and decoding" },
	{ name: "windsock", role: "Engine.IO and Socket.IO framing primitives" },
];

---

<Terminal
	title="Floodgate architecture — how the process is composed"
	description="How Floodgate composes spillway, beryl, and sibling Gleam libraries into one dual-mode Fluid Framework server."
	path="/architecture"
>
	<h1 class="page-title">architecture</h1>

	<SupervisorLog label="how a document survives a crash" id="crash" />
	<p>
		Each document session is its own BEAM process, supervised with a{" "}
		<code>one_for_one</code> restart strategy. If a session process crashes,
		it restarts and reloads its state from storage — the rest of the node,
		including every other document's session, is unaffected. The isolation
		comes from the runtime, not from Floodgate code; the sequencing and
		validation logic on top of it is written in Gleam, so a whole class of
		protocol bugs is caught by the compiler before the process ever starts.
	</p>

	<h2 class="rule-label">the process tree</h2>
	<p class="prompt">pstree floodgate</p>
	<ul class="log tree-list" data-reveal aria-label="Process tree">
		{tree.map((node, i) => (
			<li style={`--i:${i}`}>
				{node.prefix && <span class="log__tag">{node.prefix} </span>}
				<strong>{node.name}</strong>
				{node.desc && <span class="note"> — {node.desc}</span>}
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<p>
		Floodgate doesn't implement the Fluid protocol itself. It composes a
		handful of sibling Gleam libraries: some contribute workers to the process
		tree, while others provide protocol, cryptography, storage, and runtime
		utilities. Floodgate adds the transports, storage wiring, and admin surface
		around them.
	</p>

	<h2 class="rule-label">composed from</h2>
	<ul class="log" data-reveal aria-label="Sibling libraries">
		{composedFrom.map((lib, i) => (
			<li style={`--i:${i}`}>
				<span class="log__tag">[lib]</span>{" "}
				<strong><a href={`https://${lib.name}.tylerbutler.com`} rel="noopener external">{lib.name}</a></strong>
				<span class="note"> — {lib.role}</span>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<h2 class="rule-label">dual-mode, one process</h2>
	<p>
		<code>socketio_transport</code> and <code>phx_transport</code> are two
		wire-format adapters in front of the same <code>beryl</code> socket runtime,
		session state, and storage. A Fluid client on <code>/socket.io/</code>{" "}
		and a Phoenix Channels client on <code>/socket/websocket</code> can
		collaborate on the same document at the same time; neither transport
		knows the other exists.
	</p>

	<h2 class="rule-label">http surface (excerpt)</h2>
	<ul class="log tree-list" data-reveal aria-label="HTTP surface excerpt">
		{routeExcerpt.map((route, i) => (
			<li style={`--i:${i}`}>
				<span class="log__tag">{route.method}</span> <code>{route.path}</code>
				<span class="note"> — {route.desc}</span>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>
	<p class="note">
		full endpoint reference: <a href="/docs/http-surface">HTTP surface</a>
	</p>

	<hr />

	<p>
		The fastest way to check any of this is to run it: the container builds
		from source and answers on <code>/health</code> in three steps.
	</p>
	<a class="cta" href="/docs/getting-started">get Floodgate running</a>
</Terminal>
